text:
**Refined prompt:** Pre-push review of the `PUT /user` password-hashing change in `UserService.update()` — does it actually fix the change-password path (LESSONS.md #88 / CURRENT_FOCUS item 3), and does it introduce anything new? Answer as a blocking reviewer: findings ordered by severity, each cited to `file:line`, with a concrete patch and an explicit note on what I could not verify.

**Verdict: don't push.** The change is aimed at the right bug, but as written it doesn't compile, and if it did it would reproduce the very lockout it's meant to fix — plus it introduces two new problems.

---

## Blockers

**1. Missing `await` — this reintroduces #88 in a new costume**

`argon2.hash()` is async; the entity's own hook awaits it (`src/user/user.entity.ts:30`). Without `await`, `updated.password` holds a `Promise`, which TypeORM stringifies into the `varchar` column as `[object Promise]`. The next login runs `argon2.verify(user.password, password)` (`src/user/user.service.ts:31`) against a non-hash and fails forever — the exact user-locked-out outcome LESSONS.md:7–16 records, just with different garbage in the column.

**2. `UpdateUserDto` has no `password` field — two compile errors**

`src/user/dto/update-user.dto.ts:1-6` declares only `username`, `email`, `bio`, `image`. So `dto.password` is TS2339, and `updated.password = <Promise<string>>` is TS2322. `prestart:prod` runs bare `tsc` (`package.json:9`) and dev boots through `ts-node/register` with type-checking on (`index.js:1`) — both paths fail at startup, not at request time.

This is more than a type annotation. The password only reaches the service at all because nothing strips unknown body fields — the feature is currently relying on the mass-assignment hole as its input channel. Declare it.

**3. The response now leaks the password hash**

`delete toUpdate.password` (`user.service.ts:76`) was what kept the secret out of the returned object. The new line puts it back, `save()` returns that same entity, and `UserController.update()` (`user.controller.ts:26-29`) serializes it straight to the client. So `PUT /user` starts returning `password` in its body. That's a regression this diff causes — pre-existing only in the sense that the handler returns a bare entity instead of the `{user}` envelope (LESSONS.md:49–57, #57), which is what makes the field visible.

---

## The one that worries me most

`Object.assign(toUpdate, dto)` (`user.service.ts:79`) is still there, and **fixing the hashing without fixing the merge upgrades #112 from a nuisance to account takeover.**

`PUT /user` is guarded (`user.module.ts:20`) but only authenticates. A logged-in caller sending `{"user": {"id": <victim>, "password": "pwned"}}` overwrites `toUpdate.id` before `save()`, so the UPDATE lands on the victim's row. Today that writes plaintext, so nobody can log in as them — broken, self-limiting. After this change it writes a *valid* hash the attacker chose. CURRENT_FOCUS.md:13-14 deliberately bundles the two halves ("hash on update **and** stop the raw body being merged"); shipping the first half alone is worse than shipping neither.

One correction to LESSONS.md:46-47 while you're here: the whitelist-with-a-pipe option isn't available in this repo. `ValidationPipe.transform()` returns the raw `value`, not the `plainToClass` output (`src/shared/pipes/validation.pipe.ts:18-23`), so it validates without stripping. Explicit field copy is the only fix.

---

## Suggested patch

`src/user/dto/update-user.dto.ts`:
```ts
export class UpdateUserDto {
  readonly username: string;
  readonly email: string;
  readonly bio: string;
  readonly image: string;
  readonly password: string;
}
```

`src/user/user.service.ts`:
```ts
async update(id: number, dto: UpdateUserDto): Promise<UserEntity> {
  const toUpdate = await this.userRepository.findOne(id);
  delete toUpdate.password;
  delete toUpdate.favorites;

  if (dto.username !== undefined) toUpdate.username = dto.username;
  if (dto.email !== undefined)    toUpdate.email    = dto.email;
  if (dto.bio !== undefined)      toUpdate.bio      = dto.bio;
  if (dto.image !== undefined)    toUpdate.image    = dto.image;
  if (dto.password) {
    toUpdate.password = await argon2.hash(dto.password);
  }

  const updated = await this.userRepository.save(toUpdate);
  delete updated.password;
  return updated;
}
```

`argon2` is already imported at `user.service.ts:12`, so the diff needs no new import.

## Smaller notes

- **No double-hash risk** — worth stating since it's the obvious worry: `toUpdate` carries an id, so `save()` issues an UPDATE and `@BeforeInsert() hashPassword()` (`user.entity.ts:28-31`) never fires. Both call sites use default argon2 params, so hashes stay comparable.
- `if (dto.password)` correctly treats `""` as "no change", but a one-character or whitespace password passes. `CreateUserDto` only has `@IsNotEmpty()` (`create-user.dto.ts:11-12`), so registration is equally weak — fix both or neither, separately from this diff.
- Changing a password doesn't invalidate existing tokens; JWTs live 60 days (`.claude/knowledge/auth.md:11`). Out of scope here, but it means "I think someone has my password" has no remedy.
- Returning the `{user}` envelope from `PUT /user` is a genuine pre-existing bug (#57) and would also have masked finding 3. Reasonable to fold into this PR, your call.

## What I didn't verify

`node_modules` isn't installed, so I could not run `tsc`, the test suite, or the endpoint — findings 1 and 2 are read from the sources and `argon2@^0.26.2`'s async `hash` signature (`package.json:34`), not from a failing build. The takeover path is reasoned from `Object.assign` semantics plus TypeORM's id-based save; I'd want it confirmed against a real MySQL instance before treating the severity as settled. I read the user module, the shared validation pipe, and both DTOs; I did not read `auth.middleware.ts` (relied on `.claude/knowledge/auth.md`) or anything under `article/`.
